sequenceDiagram
  %% route.destroy on the Gateway engine: the steps of route/destroy.mmd (workflow operation-route-removal) as `dns` and `route` family primitives. Built for ADR 0267 and ADR 0271 (RouteDestroyEngineFlow); runtime proof pending.
  %% Reuses `dns.publish_records` for the DNS withdrawal; the site withdrawal is `route.withdraw_site`, a `route` family primitive that route/engine/update.mmd and the planned Route steps of instance/container/destroy-engine.mmd reuse.
  %% status: built
  %% command: route:destroy
  %% kind: route.destroy
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple
  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber
  %% lane V icon=server pill=executor tone=green
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS
  participant N as Route Node<br/>executor
  participant V as VPN Node<br/>DNS executor
  CLI->>GW: route:destroy ROUTE
  Note over GW: Guard, capture placements<br/>Route unchanged
  %%> Same guards and captured states as route/destroy.mmd
  Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes
  %%> Same hold as Swamp: each Node that serves the site, from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on one of them waits
  alt the engine admits the whole Operation
    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):
    %%> - `route.destroy` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`
    %%> - every Node a step touches reports its action in `$KV.NODES`: `dns.publish_records` (exists) on each VPN Node, `route.withdraw_site` on each Route Node
    %%> - each VPN Node also lists the guarded DNS input, `dns.guarded_records`, in its reported `features` (as `instance.t3_pairing` is listed), checked by EngineKinds::routes from the step's `features`; the existing decoder refuses unknown fields, so without the feature the whole removal stays on Swamp and never fails after a step acted
    %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; a part it does not cover throws `EngineUnsupported` before any order
    %%> - eligible plan: an ordinary private Route with no URL write; `--offline`, a pool change on a Node the Route does not leave, Caddy access or link writes, a never-activated replacement (it restores the old domain's URL and DNS), LAN rules, an Ingress edge, `url_lines` for a surviving Route and any `env_files` write throw `EngineUnsupported`, so the application never keeps pointing at a withdrawn name
    Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start
    %%> - the step rows and the Operation lock commit in one transaction before the start event; an Operation never moves between engines after this
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
    opt domain outside the TLD
      EN->>V: Withdraw the DNS answers<br/>dns.publish_records · dns-i
      %%> Reuses `dns.publish_records` with the records and the catalog rendered without the Route, its Router answers (`overrides`) included; other Routes' records stay byte for byte
      %%> The guarded input (behind the feature above) carries, per file, the digest the Gateway rendered from (`route_guards`, the whole files rendered from the sites at admission) and the desired bytes as a blob named by their digest
      %%> Per file: live equals desired (this step already wrote it, also one half of a records/catalog pair) is kept; live equals the rendered-from digest is replaced; anything else refuses `owned_revision_conflict` and writes nothing, so unrelated changes are never overwritten
      %%> `dnsmasq --test`, then the restart, on every run, also a replay, so files written before a crash are loaded; a failed check puts the previous files back and restarts nothing
      V-->>EN: Files and restart<br/>applied · catalog digest
      Note over EN: Wait the DNS grace<br/>31 s: TTL 30 s + 1 s
      %%> A Gateway step (`grace`) after every `dns-i`: it waits until 31 s after the last recorded DNS receipt (`done_ms`); a redelivered receipt or a resend never moves that time, and a repeat of the step waits only what is left
      %%> Differs from the draft: the wait runs in the Gateway step, not a NATS schedule, because the engine has no delayed-step primitive yet; a retry Operation is a new run, whose own DNS step (`applied: 0`) starts a full 31 s again, which is never shorter than the draft's
    end
    EN->>N: Withdraw site and leaf<br/>withdraw_site · node
    %%> One Node-local primitive under the Node target lock: the live Caddyfile must equal the digest of the one the Gateway rendered from at admission, or already the desired bytes, so every other site stays byte for byte; then the pools are written and tested, the candidate Caddyfile is validated as `caddy` and moved in place. Differs from the draft: no previous generation is kept, because a candidate that fails validation never replaces the live file. Caddy and PHP-FPM reload on every run, also a replay that finds nothing to change, as `route.publish_site` does, so a crash after the commit still makes the withdrawal live
    %%> Removes the Route's owned Caddy site block, its leaf certificate folders and, for a development target, its PHP-FPM pool share; a repeat finds them absent and changes nothing (`applied: 0`)
    %%> A live Caddyfile that matches neither digest refuses before any write (`owned_revision_conflict`)
    N-->>EN: Owned state absent<br/>site · leaf · pool read back
    %%> Verification read-back in the same primitive: the desired Caddyfile is live, no leaf folder remains, and Caddy's admin API (`127.0.0.1:2019/config/`) names none of the Route's domains (`route.withdraw_unverified`)
    EN->>N: Probe a sibling Route<br/>TLS over WireGuard
    %%> A Gateway step (`sibling-node`) as route.create's probe: a TLS handshake with another Route on the same Node, trusting only the Orbit root, proves Caddy still serves the rest; skipped when the Node serves no other Route
    opt the receipt is lost
      EN->>N: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID
      %%> When:
      %%> - the step passes its resend deadline with no receipt; two sends, one execution
      %%> - the Operation keeps its lock, so the Route and its held Nodes stays fenced; no other Operation and no Swamp run acts on it meanwhile
      %%> On the Node:
      %%> - the journal holds `done` for this Operation step, action and target: the executor answers the stored result with `repeat: true` and does not act again
      %%> - an order still `open` replays under the target lock first; an order that expired before the lock did nothing (`executor.order_expired`), and the step is sent again
      N-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true
    end
    alt every step verified
      EN->>GW: Delete the Route record<br/>free the held Nodes
      %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records
    else a step fails
      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail
      %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it
      Note over GW: Record the failure<br/>Route kept · failed step
      %%> - what was withdrawn stays withdrawn; no rollback
      %%> - retry: the same command; the DNS step repeats with the same bytes (`applied: 0`) and each Node withdrawal converges
    end
  else the engine does not admit it
    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-route-removal
    %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-route-removal (route/destroy.mmd)
    %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
  end
